tel 1.0

base abscission
import soundness

info   Shrinking a classpath to the closed world its entry points reach, by recompiling it from its TASTy
detail
    _Abscission_ takes a classpath and a set of entry points and writes the closed world they
    reach: the classes which can run, and no others. It is the job a bytecode shrinker does,
    done earlier. The classes which this compiler compiled are recompiled from the TASTy the
    classpath holds for them, and the members nothing reaches are taken out after inlining and
    before the phases which generate a trait's forwarders, a bridge or the fields behind a lazy
    value, so none of those is generated for a member which is not kept, and none needs a rule
    to keep it. Units are read as the analysis finds it needs them, so a unit nothing reaches
    is never read and its macros never run.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    def shrink(classpath: LocalClasspath, destination: Path on Linux): Shrink.Report =
      Shrink(keep = List(t"com.example.plugins.**"))
        ( classpath, List(fqcn"com.example.Main"), destination )
    ```

    Three kinds of class make up the closed world. A class this compiler compiled, and which
    is reached, is recompiled with only the members which are reached. A class this compiler
    compiled which is named only as a type, in a signature, a cast or a test, is written as a
    shell: its name, its flags and its parents. Any other class, of Java, Kotlin or the
    standard library, is copied as it is if bytecode which is kept refers to it.

    The library has three source sets. `abscission.engine` is `Shaker` and `Shrinker`,
    written against the compiler and the JDK alone and taking plain types, as
    `probably.Instrumenter` is. `abscission.core` is `Shrink`, the same with Soundness's types.
    `abscission.linker` makes shrinking a step of an Anthology `Toolchain`: `shrinkEdges`,
    `shrinkOptions` and the nodes `Shrunk` and `Shrunk.Jar`. All three are JVM-only, and need
    the compiler on the classpath of whatever runs them.

    What only reflection reaches has to be said, with `keep` and `pin`. The closed world is
    checked before it is written, on the compiler's trees and again on the bytecode, and a
    reference from anything kept to something removed raises `Shrink.Error`.

refer abscission.Shrink
refer abscission.shrinkEdges
refer abscission.Shrunk
refer abscission.Shrinker

entry .Shrink   how a classpath is shrunk: the options, and the patterns of classes to keep and to pin
  detail
      A `Shrink` is a configuration, and applying it does the work:
      `Shrink(…)(classpath, entryPoints, out)` writes the closed world of `classpath` which
      `entryPoints` reach to the directory `out`, and gives a `Shrink.Report`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      def shrink(classpath: LocalClasspath, destination: Path on Linux): Shrink.Report =
        Shrink(pin = List(t"com.example.legacy.*"), services = false)
          ( classpath, List(fqcn"com.example.Main"), destination )
      ```

      In a pattern, `*` stands for part of a name and `**` for any number of packages, so
      `com.example.plugins.**` matches every class beneath that package and
      `com.example.*Codec` every class of `com.example` whose name ends in `Codec`. A pattern
      is matched against a class's binary name, written with dots: a nested class is
      `com.example.Outer$Inner`, and an object's class ends in `$`.

      The entry points are classes with a `main` method. Each implementation which a
      `META-INF/services` file names is an entry point too, unless `services` is `false`.
  refer abscission.Shrinker
  refer abscission.shrinkEdges
  entry #options   the compiler options the classes were compiled with, beyond Soundness's own
      The recompilation runs with one set of options for the whole classpath: the options
      Soundness itself is compiled with, which are built in, followed by these. Whether a unit
      was capture-checked is read from its TASTy, and need not be said.
  entry #keep   patterns of classes to keep whole, which reflection reaches
      A class which matches is treated as instantiated, and every member of it is kept, with
      all that those members reach. It is what a class found by name at run time needs: one
      loaded with a `Class.forName` whose argument is not a literal, or read as a resource.
  entry #pin   patterns of top-level classes whose units are copied as they are
      A unit which matches is not recompiled. Its classes are copied whole, and everything
      their bytecode names in the classes which are recompiled is kept. It is the way round a
      unit the analysis gets wrong, and it is what the shrinker does of its own accord with a
      unit which cannot be recompiled.
  entry #services   whether every implementation which a `META-INF/services` file names is an entry point
      `true` unless it is changed. The files are merged across the classpath and written with
      the implementations which were kept; with `false`, an implementation is kept only if
      something else reaches it.
  entry #fine   whether a library with no TASTy is taken a class at a time
      A library of Scala is always taken a class at a time. One with no TASTy, of Java or
      Kotlin, is taken whole as soon as one class of it is reached, since such a library may
      load its own classes by name; with `fine`, it is taken a class at a time too.
  entry #apply   writes the closed world of a classpath to a directory, and reports what was done
      Raises `Shrink.Error` with reason `Unsound` if anything kept refers to something
      removed, or if a unit can be neither recompiled nor pinned, and with reason `Failed` if
      the shrinker itself fails. An entry point which is not a class of the classpath is a
      problem, not an empty result.
  entry .Report   what shrinking a classpath did, in numbers
    detail
        `units` are the units which could be recompiled from their TASTy and `read` those
        which were, because something reached them. `classes` and `size` are the classpath's,
        and `kept` and `written` the closed world's. Of the classes kept, `recompiled` came
        from TASTy, `shells` are only a name and its parents, and `copied` are as they were.
        `members` and `removed` count the methods and fields of the recompiled classes.
        `pinned` are the units which were not recompiled, whether by request or because they
        could not be, and `notes` say why of the latter. `packages` are the packages of the
        classpath, in the order of their names.
    entry #why   the chain of classes which led to a class being kept
        Each class of the chain refers to the one before it; the last is one which was
        recompiled, or was an entry point. A class which was not kept is a chain of itself.
  entry .Package   a package of the classpath, with its classes there and in the closed world
  entry .Error   raised when a classpath cannot be shrunk: error `↯/s.146.1`, _the classpath could not be shrunk because `reason`_
    entry .Reason   why: the closed world would not be sound, or the shrinker failed
      detail
          ```tabular
          column  Case
          column  Code
          column  Message
          row  `Unsound(problems)`  1  _a problem was found: `problem`_, or _`n` problems were found, of which the first was: `problem`_
          row  `Failed(trace)`  2  _the shrinker terminated abnormally_
          ```

entry .Shrunk   a closed world of classfiles: the toolchain node reached from `Classfile` through `shrinkEdges`
  detail
      An `Artifact.Ir` with id `shrunk`: a directory which is a classpath of its own, holding
      the classes the entry points reach, the resources of the libraries which gave them, and
      no TASTy.
  entry .Jar   an executable JAR of a closed world: the application node reached from `Shrunk`
      An `Artifact.Application` with id `shrunk-jar`. It is a node of its own, and not
      `Artifact.Jar`, so that a toolchain which has both `jarEdges` and `shrinkEdges` has one
      path to each.

entry .shrinkEdges   the edges which make shrinking a step of a toolchain
  detail
      `shrinkEdges(scalac)` registers two edges: from `Toolchain.Universe.Classfile` to
      `Shrunk`, and from `Shrunk` to `Shrunk.Jar`. The first carries the options of the
      `Scalac` which compiled the classes, since it compiles them again, and takes the
      toolchain's entry points as the roots of what is kept. The second packages the closed
      world as `jarEdges` packages a classpath, with at most one entry point as its
      `Main-Class`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val steps = Toolchain(shrinkEdges(Scalac[3.9](Nil))).path(Toolchain.Universe.Classfile, Shrunk.Jar).map(_.target.id)
      ```

      Here `steps` is `List(t"shrunk", t"shrunk-jar")`. A `Shrink.Error` is raised to the
      toolchain as a `Link.Error`, with reason `Packaging` for a closed world which would not
      be sound and `Failed` for a shrinker which failed.

entry .shrinkOptions   settings for the shrinking edges
  entry .keep   a pattern of the classes to keep whole, for the `Shrunk` node
  entry .pin   a pattern of the top-level classes whose units are copied as they are, for the `Shrunk` node
  entry .services   whether every implementation a `META-INF/services` file names is an entry point
  entry .fine   whether a library with no TASTy is taken a class at a time
  entry .name   the JAR's filename within the output directory, for the `Shrunk.Jar` node
      The default is `main.jar`.

entry .Shrinker   the engine: shrinks a classpath, taking and returning plain types
  detail
      What `Shrink` calls, for a host which has paths and names and wants numbers back, and
      would not have Soundness's types on its own classpath. A `Shrinker.Request` is the
      classpath's entries, the output directory, the options, the entry points and the
      patterns; `Shrinker.shrink` gives a `Shrinker.Report`, whose `problems` are empty if the
      closed world was written.
  entry .shrink   shrinks a classpath, telling `progress` what is being done as it is begun
  entry .classpath   the entries a classloader loads from, as absolute paths
      A `URLClassLoader`'s own URLs, or the JVM's `java.class.path` for any other loader.

entry .Shaker   the compiler's part: recompiles units from TASTy without what nothing reaches
  detail
      `Shaker.shake` recompiles what a set of `Shaker.Root`s reach, of the units it is told
      it may read, into a directory; `Shaker.recompile` recompiles every unit it is given and
      removes nothing. Both leave out the TASTy of what they write. `Shrinker` is what
      decides the roots and what becomes of the result.
  entry .compiler   the compiler this is, as a TASTy file records the one which wrote it
